---
/**
 * RecordUndecidability — the page's principal proof-like figure (brief §5–§6).
 *
 * THE SOURCE. *The Two Jobs of the Conditioning Bar* v1.0, §4.1–§4.2. Proposition 1 and its worked
 * collision are reproduced in the deposit's own notation and semantics — histories, the projection
 * ρ, the predicate V_j, the two edge types cond/const, and the stopping-rule pair whose displayed
 * posteriors coincide exactly at Beta(5,7) → Beta(9,13). Nothing here is a sketch invented for the
 * web; the sketch in the page brief was deliberately not implemented literally, because the canon
 * carries a more precise form.
 *
 * EQUAL-DIGNITY DESIGN LAW (brief §16). Both histories are lawful. h_B — the one that crosses a
 * constitution edge — is NOT an error path, and the deposit says so directly: "A workflow can cross
 * it freely when criticism warrants revision." So the two columns are identical in width, rule,
 * type, colour and order-of-appearance. There is no red/green, no warning glyph, no "normal vs
 * exceptional" hierarchy, and neither column carries the verdict.
 *
 * THE VERDICT SITS ON THE PROJECTION, which is where the defect actually is: ρ erases model version
 * and transition type, and that erasure — not either history — is what makes V_j unrecoverable.
 *
 * NO JAVASCRIPT. The figure is HTML and CSS. Every claim it makes is present as DOM text in reading
 * order, so the proof survives with styles off, in a text browser, and in a screen reader; the
 * chains are ordered lists because they are lineages, and the repair table is a real table.
 */
---

<section id="record-undecidability" class="ru ds ds-paper ds-section" aria-labelledby="ru-h">
  <div class="ds-measure-wide">
    <h2 id="ru-h" class="ds-label">The structural turn</h2>
    <p class="ds-proposition">Two lawful histories can leave the same record.</p>

    <p class="ru-lede ds-body">
      A Bayesian analysis is usually displayed as a relation between a parameter and the data,
      <span class="ds-mono">p(θ | X)</span>. The declaration under which that relation holds is
      normally understood and not recorded. Suppress it, and something sharper than sloppiness
      follows: two analyses that differ in what they did can become indistinguishable in what they
      show.
    </p>

    <figure class="fig">
      <figcaption class="fig-head">
        <p class="fig-label">Figure 1 · record-undecidability</p>
        <p class="fig-title">Two lawful histories. One transcript. The difference is not in the record.</p>
      </figcaption>

      <div class="pair">
        <div class="history">
          <p class="history-name">History <b>h<sub>A</sub></b> · fixed declaration</p>
          <ol class="chain">
            <li class="state"><span class="node">M₀</span></li>
            <li class="edge"><span class="t">cond(X₁)</span></li>
            <li class="state"><span class="node">M₀</span></li>
            <li class="edge"><span class="t">cond(X₂)</span></li>
            <li class="state"><span class="node">M₀</span></li>
          </ol>
          <p class="history-note">
            Every transition is conditionalization under one declared model. Bayes applies on each
            edge. Lawful.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="history">
          <p class="history-name">History <b>h<sub>B</sub></b> · declaration revised</p>
          <ol class="chain">
            <li class="state"><span class="node">M₀</span></li>
            <li class="edge"><span class="t">cond(X₁)</span></li>
            <li class="state"><span class="node">M₀</span></li>
            <li class="edge"><span class="t">const</span></li>
            <li class="state"><span class="node">M₁</span></li>
            <li class="edge"><span class="t">cond(X₁ ∪ X₂)</span></li>
            <li class="state"><span class="node">M₁</span></li>
          </ol>
          <p class="history-note">
            A stopping rule is discovered after X₁. The declaration is replaced, and the retained
            evidence re-enters under it. Equally lawful.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="converge">
        <span class="funnel" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="rho-mark" aria-hidden="true">ρ</span>
        <span class="funnel" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </div>
      <p class="rho-caption">
        ρ — a projection into a standard transcript language, suppressing model version and
        transition type
      </p>

      <div class="transcript">
        <p class="transcript-label">
          Projected transcript — <span class="mono">ρ(h<sub>A</sub>) = ρ(h<sub>B</sub>)</span>
        </p>
        <p class="transcript-line">Beta(5, 7) &nbsp;⟶&nbsp; Beta(9, 13)</p>
        <p class="verdict">
          The defect is here. Whether the displayed step was conditionalization under one unchanged
          declaration — the predicate <span class="ds-mono">V<sub>j</sub></span> — is not recoverable
          from this record. No decision function on the transcript alone can return the right answer
          for both histories.
        </p>
      </div>
    </figure>

    <div class="witness">
      <p class="witness-label">Why the collision is not a contrived one</p>
      <p class="ds-body">
        Under the initial declaration the design is fixed-<span class="ds-mono">n</span> sampling;
        under the revision, sampling continued until the eighth success. The two realized likelihoods
        differ in their combinatorial factors and agree up to proportionality in θ, so the second
        displayed posterior is <span class="ds-mono">Beta(9, 13)</span> either way. Sample spaces,
        stopping-rule declaration, predictive questions and replication semantics all differ. The
        displayed numbers do not.
      </p>
      <p class="ds-body">
        This is what “undecidable” means here, and only this: validity is
        <em>underdetermined by a projection that erases a load-bearing coordinate</em>. It is a
        statement about record semantics. It makes no claim about Turing undecidability, halting, or
        computational complexity.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .ru-lede { margin: var(--ds-space-3) 0 0; max-width: var(--ds-essay); }

  .fig { margin: var(--ds-turn) 0 0; }
  .fig-head { margin: 0 0 var(--ds-space-3); }
  .fig-label {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--ds-meta);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    margin: 0 0 var(--ds-space-1);
  }
  .fig-title {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 1.05rem + 0.9vw, 1.75rem);
    line-height: 1.24;
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 34ch;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* EQUAL DIGNITY IS LITERAL: identical column, identical rule, identical type, identical weight. */
  .pair { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 0; }
  .history {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    padding: 0 clamp(1rem, 2.5vw, 2.25rem);
    border-left: 1px solid var(--ds-rule);
  }
  .history:first-child { padding-left: 0; border-left: 0; }
  .history:last-child { padding-right: 0; }

  .history-name {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--ds-meta);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 0 0 var(--ds-space-2);
  }
  .history-name b { font-weight: 500; color: var(--ink); }

  /* a lineage reads down, not across — and stays readable at every width without reflowing */
  .chain {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--ds-mono);
  }
  .node {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 0.14rem 0.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--ink);
    background: var(--paper);
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
  }
  .chain .edge {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.55rem;
    margin-left: 0.85rem;
    padding: 0.1rem 0;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: var(--ds-mono-sm);
  }
  .chain .edge::before {
    content: "";
    width: 1px;
    height: 1.5rem;
    background: var(--ds-rule-strong);
  }

  .history-note {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    /* both notes bottom-align, so the two columns end on one line and neither reads as the
       shorter/lesser history — equal dignity is a layout fact here, not a caption. */
    margin: var(--ds-space-3) 0 0;
    padding-top: var(--ds-space-2);
  }
  .history { justify-content: flex-start; }
  .history .history-note { margin-top: auto; }

  /* the funnel: both histories collapse into one record */
  .converge {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1fr;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--ds-space-2);
    margin: var(--ds-space-4) 0 var(--ds-space-1);
  }
  .funnel { display: block; height: 1px; background: var(--ds-rule-strong); }
  .rho-mark {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 1rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    border: 1px solid var(--ds-rule-strong);
    border-radius: 50%;
    width: 1.9rem;
    height: 1.9rem;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    background: var(--paper);
  }
  /* defines ρ, so it carries the figure's meaning: --ink-soft, not --ink-faint */
  .rho-caption {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--ds-mono-sm);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    text-align: center;
    margin: 0 0 var(--ds-space-3);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .transcript {
    border: 1px solid var(--ink);
    padding: var(--ds-space-3);
    background: var(--paper-raised);
  }
  .transcript-label {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--ds-meta);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 0 0 var(--ds-space-1);
  }
  .transcript-label .mono { font-family: var(--font-mono); color: var(--ink); }
  .transcript-line {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    margin: 0;
  }
  /* The one accented line in the figure, and it is on the PROJECTION — never on a history. */
  .verdict {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ds-accent);
    margin: var(--ds-space-2) 0 0;
    max-width: 56ch;
  }

  .witness { margin: var(--ds-space-4) 0 0; max-width: var(--ds-essay); }
  .witness-label {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--ds-meta);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    margin: 0 0 var(--ds-space-2);
  }
  .witness :global(.ds-body) + :global(.ds-body) { margin-top: var(--ds-space-2); }

  @media (max-width: 47rem) {
    .pair { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .history { padding: 0; border-left: 0; }
    .history + .history {
      margin-top: var(--ds-space-4);
      padding-top: var(--ds-space-3);
      border-top: 1px solid var(--ds-rule);
    }
    .transcript { padding: var(--ds-space-2); }
  }
</style>
